Yeah. I think the strongest version is not “the net prevents the fall.” It’s “the net converts the fall into something useful.”

That gives you a much better story operator:

familiarity → attraction → edge/void → voluntary descent → capture → transformation → return

The “call of the void” becomes less about destruction and more about being drawn toward the unknown because some part of it feels strangely familiar. Then the net changes the meaning of the leap. You are not simply saved; the descent becomes harvestable.

A few story braids work especially well:

* Odysseus + the Sirens + Ariadne’s thread. The Sirens are the call. The mast is controlled exposure. Ariadne’s thread is continuity with home. Add the net at the end and now the traveler can descend into something dangerous without severing the route back—and can bring something back with him.
* The fisherman descending after the song. A fisherman hears a melody from beneath the water that sounds like a song his mother used to hum. He follows it deeper than anyone should. His fishing net, originally meant to catch food, becomes the thing that catches him—or catches whatever knowledge rises with him. Familiarity lures him; his ordinary tool becomes salvation and translation.
* The spider over the abyss. Someone keeps feeling the urge to step toward an impossible drop. Eventually they discover that every previous relationship, memory, mistake, and lesson has left a strand behind them. When they finally jump, those strands have quietly woven themselves into a web. The life behind them literally becomes the net.
* Icarus reversed. Instead of being warned never to fly too high, the protagonist is expected to fall eventually. The whole civilization secretly builds nets beneath each generation’s attempts. Every failed flight teaches the next net how to catch better. Failure becomes infrastructure.
* The familiar door. A person encounters the same door repeatedly throughout life—in dreams, childhood houses, subway stations, hospitals, wherever. One day they finally open it. Behind it is the void. They step through and discover that the “net” is made from every previous version of themselves that chose not to open the door. Their abandoned branches become the structure that catches the current one.

The deeper abstraction is probably:

Familiarity gives permission to approach the unknown.
The void supplies transformation pressure.
The net makes exploration survivable.

And the best part: the net can itself be built from prior encounters with the void.

So each cycle becomes:

approach → fall → catch → learn → strengthen net → approach farther

That is recursive luck, controlled risk, Theseus continuity, and GoalSeek all in one story.

A name for the archetype could be The Familiar Abyss, The Catch Beneath the Fall, or, probably my favorite:

The Net Under the Unknown.